.Dd June 8, 2024
.Dt LIBSOX 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libsox
.Nd audio file-format and effect library
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sox.h
.Pp
.Ft int
.Fn sox_init "void"
.Ft void
.Fn sox_quit "void"
.Ft sox_format_t *
.Fn sox_open_read "char const * path" "sox_signalinfo_t const * signal" "sox_encodinginfo_t const * encoding" "char const * filetype"
.Ft sox_format_t
.Fn sox_open_write "char const * path" "sox_signalinfo_t const * signal" "sox_encodinginfo_t const * encoding" "char const * filetype" "sox_oob_t const * oob" "sox_bool (*overwrite)(char const *)"
.Ft size_t
.Fn sox_read "sox_format_t * ft" "sox_sample_t * buf" "size_t len"
.Ft size_t
.Fn sox_write "sox_format_t * ft" "sox_sample_t const * buf" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn sox_close "sox_format_t * ft"
.Ft int
.Fn sox_seek "sox_format_t * ft" "off_t offset"
.Ft sox_effect_handler_t const *
.Fn sox_find_effect "char const * name"
.Ft sox_effect_t *
.Fn sox_create_effect "sox_effect_handler_t const * handler"
.Ft int
.Fn sox_effect_options "sox_effect_t * effp" "int argc" "char ** const argv"
.Ft sox_effects_chain_t *
.Fn sox_create_effects_chain(sox_encodinginfo_t const *\fIin_enc\fB, sox_encodinginfo_t const *\fIout_enc\fB);
.Ft void
.Fn sox_delete_effects_chain(sox_effects_chain_t *\fIecp\fB);
.Ft int
.Fn sox_add_effect(sox_effects_chaint_t *\fIchain\fB, sox_effect_t*\fIeffp\fB, sox_signalinfo_t *\fIin\fB, sox_signalinfo_t const *\fIout\fB);
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a library of sound sample file format readers/writers
and sound effects processors.
It is mainly developed for use by SoX
but is useful for any sound application.
.Pp
.Fn sox_format_init
performs initialization related to all file format handlers.
This should be called before any other file operations.
.Pp
.Fn sox_format_quit
function performs cleanup related to all file format handlers.
This should be called after all file operations are done.
.Pp
The following structures are used to specify
the audio signal properties and the encoding of samples:
.Bd -literal
typedef struct sox_signalinfo_t {
	sox_rate_t	rate;		/* samples per second		*/
	unsigned	channels;	/* number of channels		*/
	unsigned	precision;	/* bits per sample		*/
	sox_uint64_t	length;		/* samples * channels		*/
	double *	mult;		/* effects headroom multiplier	*/
} sox_signalinfo_t;
.Ed
.Bd -literal
typedef struct sox_encodinginfo_t {
	sox_encoding_t encoding;	/* encoding of samples			*/
	unsigned bits_per_sample;	/* < 0 if unknown or variable		*/
	double compression;		/* compression factor, if applicable	*/
	sox_option_t reverse_bytes;	/* defaults to format or machine	*/
	sox_option_t reverse_nibbles;	/* defaults to format or machine	*/
	sox_option_t reverse_bits;	/* defaults to format or machine	*/
	sox_bool opposite_endian;	/* reverse the format's default		*/
} sox_encodinginfo_t;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn sox_open_read
opens the named file for reading.
A special name of
.Dq -
can be used to read data from stdin.
If
.Fa signal
is not NULL, it specifies the properties of the audio signal
such as the sample rate or the number of channels.
If
.Fa encoding
is not NULL, it specifies the sample encoding.
Both
.Fa signal
and
.Fa encoding
are normally only needed for headerless audio files.
If
.Fa filetype
is not NULL, it specifies the file type.
Otherwise, the file type is guessed from
the filename extension or the file header.
.Pp
.Fn sox_open_write
opens the named file for writing.
A special name of
.Dq -
can be used to write data to stdout.
If
.Fa signal
or
.Fa encoding
are not NULL, they specify the properties of
the audio signal or the encoding of samples, as in
.Fn sox_open_read ;
for writing, this generally needs to be specified,
as most file formats can store data using various
sample rates and numbers of channels and various encodings.
The structures filled during
.Fn sox_open_read
can be used to write data in the same format that was read.
If
.Fa filetype
is not NULL, it specifies the file type.
Otherwise, the file type is guessed from the filename extension.
If
.Fa oob
is not NULL and the file formats supports comments or other out-of-bound data,
these will be stored in the file header.
If
.Fa overwrite
is not NULL and the file already exists,
the function is called, with the file name as an argument,
to determine whether the file can be overwritten.
The default is to overwrite existing files.
.Pp
.Fn sox_read
reads
.Fa len
samples into
.Fa buf
using the format handler specified by
.Fa ft .
It is the caller's responsibility to provide a large enough buffer.
Samples are converted to 32-bit signed integers before being placed in
.Fa buf .
The value of
.Fa len
is the number of samples accross all channels.
If it is not divisible by the number of channels,
the behaviour is undefined.
.Pp
.Fn sox_write
writes
.Fa len
samples from
.Fa buf
using the format handler specified by
.Fa ft .
The data in
.Fa buf
are treated as 32-bit signed integers
and are converted as specified by the format.
The value of
.Fa len
is the number of samples across all channels.
If it is not divisible by the number of channels,
the behaviour is undefined.
.Pp
.Fn sox_close
dissociates the given
.Fa sox_format_t
pointer from its underlying file and set of functions.
If the format handler was being used for output,
any buffered data is written first.
.Fn sox_seek
repositions the offset of the file associated with
.Fa ft
to the given
.Fa offset .
.Pp
.Fn sox_find_effect
finds and effect by the given
.Fa name ,
returning a pointer to its handler.
.Fn sox_create_effect
instantiates an effect into a
.Ft sox_effect_t
given its handler.
.Fn sox_effect_options
passes options to the effect to control its behavior.
.\"It will return SOX_EOF if there were any invalid options passed in.
If successful,
.Fa effp->in_signal
will optionally contain the rate and channel count
the ffect expects on input and
.Fa effp->out_signal
will optionally contain the rate and channel count of the output.
When present, this information should be used to make sure
appropriate effects are placed in the effects chain
to handle any needed conversions.
Passing in options is only supported before the effect is ever started.
Once the effect is started, the behavior is undefined.
.Pp
\fBsox_create_effects_chain\fR will instantiate an effects chain that
effects can be added to.  \fIin_enc\fR and \fIout_enc\fR are the 
signal encoding of the input and output of the chain respectively.
The pointers to \fIin_enc\fR and \fIout_enc\fR
are stored internally and so their memory should not be freed.  Also,
it is OK if their values change over time to reflect new input or
output encodings as they are referenced only as effects
start up or are restarted.
.Pp
\fBsox_delete_effects_chain\fR will release any resources reserved during
the creation of the chain.  This will also call \fBsox_delete_effects\fR
if any effects are still in the chain.
.Pp
\fBsox_add_effect\fR adds an effect to the chain.  \fIin\fR specifies the input
signal info for this effect.  \fIout\fR is a suggestion
as to what the output signal should be but depending on the effects
given options and on \fIin\fR the effect can choose to do differently.
Whatever output rate and channels the effect does produce are written
back to \fIin\fR.  It is meant that \fIin\fR be stored and passed to each
new call to \fBsox_add_effect\fR so that changes will be propagated to each new effect.
.Pp
SoX includes skeleton C files to assist you in writing new
formats (skelform.c) and effects (skeleff.c). Note that new formats 
can often just deal with the header and then use raw.c's routines 
for reading and writing.
.Pp
example0.c and example1.c are a good starting point to see how
to write applications using libsox.  sox.c itself is also a good
reference.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sox_open_read
and
.Fn sox_open_write
return a pointer to a filled
.Ft sox_format_t
structure, which can be passed to the readers and writers.
If an error occurs, NULL is returned.
.Pp
.Fn sox_read
and
.Fn sox_write
return the number of samples successfully read or written.
This can be less than the requested
.Fa len
when short-reading towards the end of the file.
If the end of file is reached, or an error occurs, SOX_EOF is returned.
Note that no distinction is made between end of file and an error.
.Pp
.Fn sox_close
returns
.Dv SOX_SUCCESS
on success and
.Dv SOX_EOF
on error.
In either case, any further access, including another call to
.Fn sox_close ,
will result in undefined behavior.
.Pp
.Fn sox_seek
returns 0 on success and SOX_EOF on error.
.Pp
returning a pointer to the named effect's
.Ft sox_effect_handler_t
if the effect exists, and NULL otherwise.
.\" FIXME The following has no place in RETURN VALUES
.Pp
SoX's formats and effects operate with an internal sample format of
signed 32-bit integer.  The data processing routines are called with
buffers of these samples, and buffer sizes which refer to the number
of samples processed, not the number of bytes.  File readers translate
the input samples to signed 32-bit integers and return the number of
samples read.  For example, data in linear signed byte format is
left-shifted 24 bits.
.P
Representing samples as integers can cause problems when processing the audio.  
For example, if an effect to
mix down left and right channels into one monophonic channel
were to use the line
.EX
   *obuf++ = (*ibuf++ + *ibuf++)/2;
.EE
distortion might occur since
the intermediate addition can overflow 32 bits.
The line
.EX
   *obuf++ = *ibuf++/2 + *ibuf++/2;
.EE
would get round the overflow problem (at the expense of the least significant
bit).
.P
Stereo data is stored with the left and right speaker data in
successive samples.
Quadraphonic data is stored in this order: 
left front, right front, left rear, right rear.
.Pp
A 
.I format 
is responsible for translating between sound sample files
and an internal buffer.  The internal buffer is store in signed longs
with a fixed sampling rate.  The 
.I format
operates from two data structures:
a format structure, and a private structure.
.P
The format structure contains a list of control parameters for
the sample: sampling rate, data size (8, 16, or 32 bits),
encoding (unsigned, signed, floating point, etc.), number of sound channels.
It also contains other state information: whether the sample file
needs to be byte-swapped, whether sox_seek() will work, its suffix,
its file stream pointer, its 
.I format
pointer, and the 
.I private
structure for the 
.I format .
.P
The 
.I private 
area is just a preallocated data array for the 
.I format
to use however it wishes.  
It should have a defined data structure
and cast the array to that structure.  
See voc.c for the use of a private data area.  
Voc.c has to track the number of samples it 
writes and when finishing, seek back to the beginning of the file
and write it out.
The private area is not very large.
The ``echo'' effect has to malloc() a much larger area for its
delay line buffers.
.P
A 
.I format
has 6 routines:
.TP 20
startread
Set up the format parameters, or read in
a data header, or do what needs to be done.
.TP 20
read
Given a buffer and a length: 
read up to that many samples, 
transform them into signed long integers,
and copy them into the buffer.
Return the number of samples actually read.
.TP 20
stopread
Do what needs to be done.
.TP 20
startwrite
Set up the format parameters, or write out 
a data header, or do what needs to be done.
.TP 20
write
Given a buffer and a length: 
copy that many samples out of the buffer,
convert them from signed longs to the appropriate
data, and write them to the file.
If it can't write out all the samples,
fail.
.TP 20
stopwrite
Fix up any file header, or do what needs to be done.
.Pp
Each effect runs with one input and one output stream.
An effect's implementation comprises six functions that may be called
to the follow flow diagram:
.EX
LOOP (invocations with different parameters)
  getopts
  LOOP (invocations with the same parameters)
    LOOP (channels)
      start
    LOOP (whilst there is input audio to process)
      LOOP (channels)
        flow
    LOOP (whilst there is output audio to generate)
      LOOP (channels)
        drain
    LOOP (channels)
      stop
  kill
.EE
Notes: For some effects, some of the functions may not be needed and can
be NULL.  An effect that is marked `MCHAN' does not use the LOOP
(channels) lines and must therefore perform multiple channel processing
inside the affected functions.  Multiple effect instances may be
processed (according to the above flow diagram) in parallel.
.TP 20
getopts
is called with a character string argument list for the effect.
.TP 20
start
is called with the signal parameters for the input and output
streams.
.TP 20 
flow
is called with input and output data buffers,
and (by reference) the input and output data buffer sizes.
It processes the input buffer into the output buffer,
and sets the size variables to the numbers of samples
actually processed.
It is under no obligation to read from the input buffer or
write to the output buffer during the same call.  If the
call returns SOX_EOF then this should be used as an indication
that this effect will no longer read any data and can be used
to switch to drain mode sooner.
.TP 20 
drain
is called after there are no more input data samples.
If the effect wishes to generate more data samples
it copies the generated data into a given buffer
and returns the number of samples generated.
If it fills the buffer, it will be called again, etc.
The echo effect uses this to fade away.
.TP 20
stop
is called when there are no more input samples and no more output
samples to process.
It is typically used to release or close resources (e.g. allocated
memory or temporary files) that were set-up in
.IR start .
See echo.c for an example.
.TP 20
kill
is called to allow resources allocated by
.I getopts
to be released.
See pad.c for an example.
